Merge Sort

Optimal Solution

Approach:
Divide the array into two halves until each subarray contains only one element.
Recursively sort the left half.
Recursively sort the right half.
Merge the two sorted halves into a single sorted array.
Copy the merged elements back into the original array.
Continue this process until the entire array is sorted.

Idea: Follow the Divide and Conquer approach—divide the array into smaller parts, sort each part recursively, and merge the sorted parts.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
The array is divided into halves in log n levels.
At each level, merging takes O(n) time.
Overall complexity = O(n log n).

Space Complexity: O(n)
An extra temporary array is used during the merge step to store sorted elements.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> mergeSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        mergeSort1(nums, 0, n - 1);
        return nums;
    }

    void mS(vector<int>& arr, int low, int mid, int high) {
        vector<int> temp;
        int left = low;
        int right = mid + 1;

        while (left <= mid && right <= high) {
            if (arr[left] <= arr[right]) {
                temp.push_back(arr[left]);
                left++;
            } else {
                temp.push_back(arr[right]);
                right++;
            }
        }

        while (left <= mid) {
            temp.push_back(arr[left]);
            left++;
        }

        while (right <= high) {
            temp.push_back(arr[right]);
            right++;
        }

        for (int i = low; i <= high; i++) {
            arr[i] = temp[i - low];
        }
    }

    void mergeSort1(vector<int>& arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high) return;

        int mid = (low + high) / 2;

        mergeSort1(arr, low, mid);
        mergeSort1(arr, mid + 1, high);

        mS(arr, low, mid, high);
    }
};


Key Takeaway:

"Divide the array → Sort both halves recursively → Merge the sorted halves → Repeat until the whole array is sorted."
